.TH CRABMON 1 "2026-09-13" "crabmon 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crabmon \- terminal system monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crabmon
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crabmon
is a keyboard-driven terminal system monitor. It shows per-core CPU history,
memory and swap, a sortable and filterable process table with an optional tree
view and per-process disk IO, network throughput, per-mount disk usage and IO,
temperature sensors, GPU utilisation and cgroup limits.
.PP
With
.B \-\-once
it prints a single snapshot as JSON or CSV and exits, which makes it usable
from scripts and monitoring pipelines.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-refresh " " \fIMS\fR
Refresh interval in milliseconds (200\-10000). Values below 200 ms are clamped:
the underlying sampler needs at least that long between CPU samples for the
percentages to mean anything.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort " " \fIKEY\fR
Initial sort column: \fBpid\fR, \fBname\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBvirt\fR,
\fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBthreads\fR or \fBnice\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-ascending
Sort ascending.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-descending
Sort descending. This is the default; the flag exists to override a config file
that asks for ascending.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIQUERY\fR
Initial process filter. See \fBFILTER LANGUAGE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tree
Start in process-tree view.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-layout " " \fINAME\fR
Initial layout: \fBdashboard\fR, \fBprocesses\fR, \fBcpu\fR or \fBio\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-theme " " \fINAME\fR
Colour theme: \fBdefault\fR, \fBmono\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBsolarized\fR or
\fBgruvbox\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour. Equivalent to \fB\-\-theme mono\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Do not capture mouse events.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Print one snapshot to standard output and exit instead of starting the TUI.
Always samples the host it runs on; \fB\-\-remote\fR and \fB\-\-replay\fR are
ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-format " " \fIFMT\fR
Output format for \fB\-\-once\fR: \fBjson\fR (default) or \fBcsv\fR.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-top " " \fIN\fR
Limit \fB\-\-once\fR and \fBP\fR snapshot output to the top \fIN\fR processes.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Use an alternate config file.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group " " \fIBY\fR
Aggregate the process table: \fBnone\fR, \fBservice\fR, \fBcontainer\fR or
\fBuser\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-record " " \fIPATH\fR
Record every sample to a JSON Lines file, replacing any file already at that
path. See \fBRECORDINGS\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-replay " " \fIPATH\fR
Drive the interface from a recording instead of sampling this host.
.TP
.B \-\-remote " " \fITARGET\fR
Monitor \fITARGET\fR over SSH. crabmon must be installed on the far end and
SSH must work non-interactively; the refresh rate is bounded by the round trip.
.TP
.B \-\-remote\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Command to run on the remote host. Defaults to
.BR "crabmon \-\-once \-\-refresh 200" .
.TP
.B \-\-serve " " \fIADDR\fR
Run headless and serve Prometheus metrics on \fIADDR\fR, e.g. \fB:9100\fR.
Routes: \fB/\fR, \fB/metrics\fR, \fB/healthz\fR. Always samples this host;
combining it with \fB\-\-remote\fR or \fB\-\-replay\fR is an error.
.PP
A bare port binds to loopback; \fB:\fIPORT\fR binds to every interface. The
per-process series carry command lines, so the difference matters. Connections
are handled concurrently and capped; past the cap callers get 503.
.TP
.B \-\-stream
Print one JSON snapshot per line, forever, trimmed by \fB[record]\fR exactly as
a recording is. This is what \fB\-\-remote\fR runs on the far end, and it is
usable on its own as a collector. Samples this host only.
.TP
.B \-\-diff " " \fIA\fR " " [\fIB\fR]
Compare two snapshots and print what changed: CPU, load, memory, swap,
per-filesystem usage, and the processes that started, exited and moved. Each
path may be a \fB\-\-once\fR export or a recording, in which case its last
frame is used. With one path, the recording's first frame is compared against
its last. \fB\-\-format json\fR emits the report as JSON.
.PP
Processes are matched on PID \fIand\fR start time, so a recycled PID reads as
one process exiting and another starting rather than as enormous growth.
.TP
.B \-\-watch " " \fIQUERY\fR
Sample headlessly until processes match \fIQUERY\fR (see \fBFILTER LANGUAGE\fR),
then print them and exit \fB1\fR. Exits \fB0\fR if \fB\-\-watch\-timeout\fR
elapses first. A match is the non-zero exit, so \fB||\fR runs on a match and
\fB&&\fR on a clean run.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-for " " \fISECS\fR
Require the match to hold this many consecutive seconds. A condition that
flickers in and out never accumulates credit across the gaps. Default 0.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-timeout " " \fISECS\fR
Give up after this long. 0, the default, waits forever.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q, Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.TP
.B Up/Down, k/j, PgUp/PgDn, Home/End
Move the selection.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the process detail pane, or — in the grouped view — open the selected
group, filtering the process table down to the processes inside it.
.TP
.B /
Filter processes. \fBEsc\fR clears the filter.
.TP
.B c, m, p, n, d
Sort by CPU, memory, PID, name or disk IO. Pressing the active column's key
again reverses the order.
.TP
.BR < ", " > " (or " , " and " . )
Move to the previous or next sort column.
.TP
.B s
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.B T
Toggle the process tree.
.TP
.B H
Show or hide individual threads.
.TP
.B G
Cycle grouping: none, systemd unit, container, user. The aggregate table has its
own cursor; process actions are refused from it, since it shows no process rows.
.TP
.B Space
Tag the selected process. Signals, renice and affinity then apply to every
tagged process instead of just the cursor.
.TP
.B U
Untag everything.
.TP
.B f
Pin the selected process. Pinned rows are held at the top of the table whatever
the sort, and stay visible through a filter that would otherwise hide them, so
one process can be watched while the rest churns.
.TP
.B F
Unpin everything.
.TP
.B z
Pause and resume sampling.
.TP
.BR [ ", " ]
Step a recording back or forward one frame;
.BR { " and " }
step ten.
.TP
.B 1-9
Apply the correspondingly numbered saved filter;
.B 0
clears the filter.
.TP
.B y
Copy the selected process's command line to the system clipboard using OSC 52,
which works through SSH and tmux.
.TP
.B L
Show the local log of actions crabmon has taken.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " PgUp ", " PgDn " (in a popup)"
Scroll it. The key list, the alert log and the action log are all longer than a
short terminal can show; the title says which part is on screen.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift\-Tab
Cycle layouts.
.TP
.B v
Include or exclude virtual interfaces (loopback, bridges, veth, VPN tunnels)
from the aggregate network rate.
.TP
.B g
Group or expand temperature sensors.
.TP
.BR + ", " \- " (or " = " and " _ )
Refresh faster or slower.
.TP
.B t
Signal menu for the selected process (Unix). A confirmation prompt follows.
.TP
.B r
Change the selected process's nice value (Unix).
.TP
.B A
Set the selected process's CPU affinity (Linux).
.TP
.B P
Write a snapshot to the export directory.
.TP
.B !
Show the alert rules and their state.
.TP
.BR ? " (or " F1 )
Show all key bindings.
.SH FILTER LANGUAGE
Terms are separated by whitespace and combined with AND. A leading \fB!\fR
negates a term.
.PP
.nf
firefox            name contains "firefox"
!kworker           name does not contain "kworker"
user:vlad          user name contains "vlad"
pid:1234           exact PID
ppid:1             exact parent PID
state:R            process state letter
service:sshd       systemd unit; service:\- means "no unit"
container:abc123   container id; container:\- means "not in one"
cmd:\-\-headless     substring of the full command line
re:^chrom(e|ium)$  regular expression over the process name
cpu>5              CPU percentage comparison (> >= < <= =)
mem>100M           resident memory comparison, with K/M/G/T suffixes
io>1M              disk IO rate comparison
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/crabmon/config.toml
(usually
.IR ~/.config/crabmon/config.toml ).
The file is written when the refresh interval changes and on exit. The active
filter is session state and is not written back, so a query typed at
.B /
does not silently narrow later runs. Missing or malformed files fall back to
defaults; out-of-range values are clamped rather than rejected.
.SS Top-level keys
.TP
.B refresh_ms
Refresh interval in milliseconds, clamped to 200\-10000. Default 800.
.TP
.B sort_by
Sort column: \fBpid\fR, \fBname\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBvirt\fR,
\fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBthreads\fR, \fBnice\fR.
Default \fBcpu\fR.
.TP
.B sort_desc
Sort descending. Default true.
.TP
.B filter
Initial filter query. Default empty.
.TP
.B tree
Start in process-tree view. Default false.
.TP
.B show_threads
List individual threads alongside processes. Default false: on Linux the
sampler reports every task, which is several times more rows than processes.
.TP
.B group_by
Aggregate the process table: \fBnone\fR, \fBservice\fR, \fBcontainer\fR or
\fBuser\fR. Default \fBnone\fR.
.TP
.B layout
\fBdashboard\fR, \fBprocesses\fR, \fBcpu\fR or \fBio\fR. Default
\fBdashboard\fR.
.TP
.B theme
\fBdefault\fR, \fBmono\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBsolarized\fR or \fBgruvbox\fR.
.TP
.B group_sensors
Collapse temperature sensors to one row per driver. Default true.
.TP
.B show_virtual_ifaces
Count loopback, bridges, veth pairs and tunnels in the aggregate network rate.
Default false, so VPN traffic is not counted twice.
.TP
.B virtual_iface_prefixes
Interface-name prefixes treated as virtual. Defaults to \fBlo\fR, \fBdocker\fR,
\fBbr\-\fR, \fBveth\fR, \fBvirbr\fR, \fBtun\fR, \fBtap\fR, \fBwg\fR,
\fBvmnet\fR, \fBzt\fR, \fBcni\fR, \fBflannel\fR, \fBkube\fR and \fButun\fR.
.TP
.B history_len
Samples kept per chart, clamped to 16\-4096. Default 240.
.TP
.B panel_order
Draw order of the right-hand column. Default
.BR net ", " sensors ", " disks ", " gpu ", " cgroup .
.SS [thresholds]
.B warn
and
.B crit
are the 0..1 ratios at which gauges turn yellow and red (0.7 and 0.9).
.B temp_warn
and
.B temp_crit
are the same, as a fraction of each sensor's critical temperature (0.75 and
0.9).
.SS [panels]
.BR header ", " cpu ", " mem ", " procs ", " net ", " sensors ", " disks ", "
.BR gpu ", " cgroup ", " psi " and " power
each switch a panel off when set to false. All default to true. The
.BR psi ", " power ", " gpu " and " cgroup
panels are additionally hidden when the machine has nothing to report.
.SS [gpu]
.B enabled
draws the GPU panel.
.B nvidia_smi
additionally shells out to \fBnvidia\-smi\fR on every refresh; off by default
because it costs tens of milliseconds.
.SS [export]
.B dir
is where \fBP\fR writes snapshots (empty means the working directory),
.B format
is \fBjson\fR or \fBcsv\fR, and
.B top_n
limits the process list (0 keeps every process).
.SS [record]
.B top_n
processes are kept per frame, busiest first, and real processes are preferred
over threads (default 100; 0 keeps everything, at roughly 1.5 MB a frame).
.B max_cmd_len
truncates command lines (default 200) and
.B omit_paths
drops them, along with executable paths and working directories, entirely.
.SS [procs]
.B columns
fixes the column order instead of fitting the terminal width. Names are
\fBmark\fR, \fBpid\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBtrend\fR,
\fBmem\fR, \fBvirt\fR, \fBthr\fR, \fBni\fR, \fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR,
\fBports\fR and \fBname\fR; COMMAND is always drawn last and unknown names are
ignored. Empty (the default) fits as many as will go.
.B ports
looks up listening ports for the rows on screen — one fd table per visible row,
so it is off by default.
.B pin_marker
draws the leading marker column (\fB\(bu\fR tagged, pinned).
.SS [remote]
.B stream
holds one SSH session open and reads a stream of frames instead of starting a
process per sample, falling back automatically when the far end predates
\fB\-\-stream\fR. Default true.
.SS [serve]
.B top_procs
per-process series are exported by \fB\-\-serve\fR, busiest first (default
20; 0 disables them).
.SS [audit]
.B enabled
records every signal, renice and affinity change; \fBpath\fR overrides the
default location under the platform state directory.
.SS [[filter_preset]]
Each table binds a
.B name
and a
.B query
to the next number key, in order. \fB$USER\fR is expanded. At most nine are
used, since there are only nine number keys. Declaring any preset replaces the
built-in five \(em \fBbusy\fR (\fBcpu>5\fR), \fBhungry\fR (\fBmem>500M\fR),
\fBmine\fR (\fBuser:$USER\fR), \fBio\fR (\fBio>100K\fR) and \fBstuck\fR
(\fBstate:D\fR) \(em rather than adding to them.
.SS [colors]
Overrides individual theme colours by name:
.BR ok ", " warn ", " crit ", " accent ", " border ", " title ", " text ", "
.BR dim ", " sel_fg ", " sel_bg .
Values may be colour names (\fBred\fR), hex (\fB#ff8800\fR) or 256-colour
indexes (\fB33\fR). Unknown keys and unparseable values are ignored.
.SS [[alert]]
Each table defines one rule:
.IR name ", " kind " (" cpu ", " mem ", " swap ", " load ", " disk ", " temp ", "
.IR gpu "), " threshold ", " for_secs ", " target ", " command " and "
.IR command_clear .
A rule fires only once its condition has held for \fIfor_secs\fR seconds, and
its \fIcommand\fR runs once per activation. \fIcommand_clear\fR runs once when
it recovers, and the alert popup (\fB!\fR) logs every transition,
so an alert that fired at 03:12 and cleared at 03:14 says both.
.I target
restricts disk rules to a mount point, temperature rules to a sensor prefix and
GPU rules to a device name.
.SH RECORDINGS
.B \-\-record
writes one JSON object per sample, so a recording is greppable with
.BR jq (1)
and replayable with
.BR "crabmon \-\-replay" .
Frames are trimmed by \fB[record]\fR — by default the busiest 100 processes
with command lines truncated to 200 characters, roughly 55 KiB per frame, or
about 240 MiB for an hour at the default interval.
.PP
While replaying, sampling is paused: \fB[\fR and \fB]\fR step one frame,
\fB{\fR and \fB}\fR step ten, and \fBz\fR plays on. Every panel renders
from the recording exactly as it would from the live host.
.SH PLATFORM NOTES
Sending signals and renice are Unix-only. CPU affinity, GPU statistics
(\fI/sys/class/drm\fR), cgroup limits, process grouping, open-socket lookup and
pressure stall information are Linux-only; those panels are simply absent
elsewhere. Pressure needs Linux 4.20+ with \fBCONFIG_PSI=y\fR.
.PP
Per-disk IO comes from \fI/proc/diskstats\fR on Linux and from
.BR iostat (8)
on FreeBSD. The FreeBSD path is compile-checked and unit-tested against captured
output but has not been exercised on real hardware.
.PP
Battery and mains state need no privileges. CPU package power comes from RAPL,
whose counters are root-only on most kernels since CVE-2020-8694; the figure is
omitted rather than guessed when they cannot be read. Per-process disk IO
requires permission to read \fI/proc/<pid>/io\fR, so figures for other users'
processes read as zero unless crabmon runs as root.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For \fB\-\-watch\fR, this means the condition was never met before
\fB\-\-watch\-timeout\fR elapsed.
.TP
.B 1
A \fB\-\-watch\fR condition matched, or the run failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/crabmon/config.toml
Configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR htop (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR iostat (1)
